module v2.workflow.floor_cost_debt

import v2.std.algebra { Cons, Empty, list_flat_map, any }
import v2.std.collection { List }

// THE OVER-COST WITHHOLDING MECHANISM. ITS POPULATION IS NOT STATED HERE -- ASK
// `floor_cost_debt_population_report`, WHICH FOLDS THE SAME CHUNKS THE ROSTER DOES.
//
// The count used to be stated here, and it rotted twice: "CARRYING NO IDENTITIES" against a
// populated roster, then 274 in this header and 225 + 51 = 276 in the population block against a
// roster that held 290. Three figures, no two agreeing, none of them the roster's length -- and
// the roster itself was correct throughout. A number transcribed beside its producer decays
// without anyone touching either end, so this file stopped transcribing it rather than correcting
// it a third time. The producer and the reason it carries no size witness are at
// `floor_cost_debt_population_report` below. An earlier
// revision of this header read "CURRENTLY CARRYING NO IDENTITIES" -- true of the empty carrier
// that landed first, false from the moment the roster was enrolled, and it then stood in direct
// contradiction with the population block below on the same authority. Caught by review 57235.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT THE EXPECTED-RED ROSTER, stated first because the two are easy to fuse.
//
// Any identity enrolled here is withheld FOR COST, and in none of the three evidence classes is it
// a known red. The PROVEN class passes and its measured marginal CPU exceeds
// `v2.workflow.required_floor` `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`; the CENSORED class was
// preempted before a verdict, so it asserts only that the row did not finish inside the ceiling
// on the charged clock. NEITHER is a failure, which is the distinction that matters here.
//
// THIS SENTENCE ASSERTED THE PROVEN CASE OVER THE WHOLE ROSTER UNTIL REVIEW 57258. It read "any
// identity enrolled here is a witness that PASSES and whose MARGINAL cost exceeds" the ceiling --
// written when the carrier held only the proven class, and left standing when the censored class
// was enrolled. The file then contradicted itself at its most-read point: this line claimed every
// member passes, while the population block below states the censored rows reach no verdict and
// that most of them have no established own-work cost. Same class as the "CARRYING NO IDENTITIES"
// header this file already corrected once -- a universal that was true of an earlier population
// and was never re-read when the population grew.
//
// Either way it is a different fact from a known red, and may not ride the same carrier:
// `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red`'s own header states the bar it enforces -- "ENROLLING AN
// IDENTITY ASSERTS THAT IT REACHES ITS SUBJECT AND ANSWERS" -- and a withheld row answers
// nothing. Enrolling a green over-cost row there would assert a red that does not exist.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT BASIS IS MARGINAL COST, AND THAT SENTENCE IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS ROSTER WAS
// REBUILT ONCE BEFORE IT LANDED. The first cut of this population was discovered from main run
// 33109071348 at aea5e0dfd (15:34). `v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_claim_measured` began SUBTRACTING
// shared-artifact fill from a claim's charged CPU at gunbc#9477, which landed at 18:49 -- three
// hours after that measurement. So the first roster was built from CHARGED figures, in which a
// claim that happened to touch a shared artifact first was billed the entire fill.
//
// THE ERROR WAS NOT SMALL AND IT WAS NOT UNIFORM: of the 320 identities that population held,
// 119 -- 37% -- measure at or under the ceiling once fill is attributed to the artifact instead.
// The extremes are the tell. `test.claim.alias_identity_across_unwrap_witness`
// `a_second_alias_still_emits_its_own_name_and_not_the_first` was the SECOND most expensive row
// in the corpus at 4250ms charged; its marginal cost is 0ms. It never cost anything. Freezing it
// would have deleted a working witness's coverage, at identity grain, under a shrink-only
// contract that makes a wrong entry expensive to walk back -- and it would have looked exactly
// like a correct entry from the inside, because the number was real.
//
// So the universe was rediscovered from main run 33131296988 (b6003a45e), the first completed
// floor measurement after gunbc#9477. A future rebuild must use a post-#9477 measurement for the
// same reason; a charged-basis figure is not this roster's subject and never was.
//
// AND THEN FOURTEEN MORE WERE ADDED, from the first run that executed this mechanism at all
// (33136345225). That is the initial census completing rather than the roster growing, and the
// distinction is worth stating because this contract is shrink-only and an unexplained addition
// would look like a violation of it. Those fourteen are rows the 500ms ceiling caught that main's
// own 5000ms run could not have reported: seven were INTERRUPTED at 501-508ms CPU, having never
// reached a verdict, and seven completed over. A row cannot be discovered as over-ceiling by a
// measurement taken under a ceiling ten times looser, so the population was only fully knowable
// once the ceiling it is defined against was the one actually running. From here the direction is
// shrink-only in earnest.
//
// SIXTEEN FURTHER ROWS FAILED THAT RUN AND ARE DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. They exceeded on WALL while
// their CPU sat inside the ceiling, because fill is netted out of CPU and not out of wall, so a
// first-touch payer reads ~0ms CPU and a full shared-build wall. Rostering them would have frozen
// working witnesses for a defect in the wall clock's derivation -- the same error as the
// charged-basis roster, one clock over. They were fixed by re-deriving
// `required_floor_claim_wall_safety_limit_ms` against the population it actually has to clear;
// that constant carries the receipt.
//
// THE OPERATOR RULING THIS SERVES (2026-08-27). The per-claim ceiling is 500ms. It was set to
// 500 at gunbc#8283 (2026-08-17) and was raised twice without a ruling on the value: to 1552 at
// gunbc#8514, justified only by "measured-max plus one scheduler-quantum" over the then-current
// tree -- the tree-copied oracle DESIGN section 5 forbids -- and to 5000 at gunbc#8533, which
// cited an operator ruling that was about SPLITTING the CPU and wall clocks, not about the
// magnitude. The operator's quoted words in that diff, "DO NOT set CPU and wall to the same
// 5000ms", presuppose 5000 was already on the table; the value rode in as a passenger on a
// ruling about something else. That is the AUTHORITY SUBSTITUTION failure mode DESIGN names.
// The ruling of 2026-08-17 was never repealed. This roster restores it.
//
// WHAT MEMBERSHIP MEANS, STATED SO IT IS NEVER READ AS COVERAGE. A rostered identity is
// WITHHELD FROM EXECUTION. It does not run on the floor, it does not run in a long home, it
// does not run on a cadence. Its coverage is GONE while it sits here. That is a declared
// DESIGN section 4b(3) rung drop -- below -- and not a deferral: the operator's ruling is that
// these rows are broken, and a broken row does not get an exemption that lets it keep spending
// the budget it broke.
//
// WHY IN PLACE RATHER THAN MOVED TO test/claim/long/. Relocation is the move the 2026-08-04
// admission ruling forbids and that `gunbc.witness_deferral_freeze` exists to close: it deletes
// coverage while retaining the source, with nothing counting what was lost (the gunbc#7762
// specimen). The distinction is not the coverage outcome -- both stop executing -- it is that
// this roster is DECLARED, COUNTED EVERY RUN, IDENTITY-GRAIN, AND SHRINK-ONLY, and a relocation
// is none of those. The files do not move.
//
// THE MONOTONE DEBT CONTRACT (DESIGN section 5's fourth sanctioned oracle form), and each
// condition is load-bearing:
//   - the subject universe is INDEPENDENTLY DISCOVERED -- the floor measures every claim's
//     marginal cost itself; this roster is checked against that discovery, never authored beside
//     it;
//   - membership is at IDENTITY GRAIN, never a count. No witness asserts this roster's size
//     against a literal, because a number copied from the current tree is a snapshot;
//   - the direction is justified: a row leaves by being made cheap, and the forward hole is
//     already shut -- a NEW or REGRESSED row over 500ms simply refuses, because 500 is the
//     ceiling and nothing here admits an identity the floor did not already discover;
//   - a rostered identity the tree no longer carries REFUSES as a stale row, so the roster
//     cannot rot into a permission slip for witnesses that no longer exist.
//
// A KNOWN, BOUNDED IMPURITY IN THIS UNIVERSE, DECLARED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED LATER.
//
// The marginal basis above corrects attribution for ONE shared artifact and not for shared
// builds in general. `v1_compiler.cli_run` `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` has exactly one
// call site -- inside `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` -- so a claim whose cost is dominated by a
// DIFFERENT unbracketed shared build is still charged for work it does not own, and the
// subtraction reports zero for it. The known instance: gunbc#9428 memoized the sibling
// `compile_dag_diagnostic_census` without a bracket, so a census miss accumulates nothing.
// (Found by snappy-dove-250 and sleek-cat-45; the single call site and the census's missing
// bracket are verified here by code read, and the discriminating evidence is theirs -- main run
// 33131296988 carries 259 per-claim `[floor-shared-fill]` lines and none names a
// callable_candidate_ambiguity row, beside a 5812ms charge that is almost entirely fill.)
//
// SIXTEEN OF THE ROWS BELOW SIT IN A CENSUS-CALLING MODULE, and they are named so that this is
// a retryable list rather than an invisible one:
//   test.claim.arity_zero_import_refusal_witness.the_same_import_carrying_one_member_is_accepted
//   test.claim.callable_candidate_ambiguity_witness.a_bare_call_whose_only_declarer_is_reached_transitively_refuses
//   test.claim.callable_candidate_ambiguity_witness.a_listed_import_does_not_exclude_a_transitively_reached_homonym
//   test.claim.callable_candidate_ambiguity_witness.neither_green_source_refuses_and_neither_mis_resolves
//   test.claim.declared_type_inhabitance_direct_call_witness.w_kernel_numeric_at_the_natively_realized_nat_is_admitted
//   test.claim.declared_type_inhabitance_direct_call_witness.w_kernel_numeric_at_the_peano_nat_is_admitted_through_its_homomorphism
//   test.claim.declared_type_inhabitance_list_element_witness.w_declared_coproduct_member_is_accepted
//   test.claim.declared_type_inhabitance_list_element_witness.w_optional_carrier_is_undecidable_not_refused
//   test.claim.declared_type_inhabitance_list_element_witness.w_reachability_control_discriminates_on_the_name
//   test.claim.generic_lambda_parameter_binding_witness.a_lambda_argument_binds_a_type_variable_a_later_argument_determines
//   test.claim.generic_lambda_parameter_binding_witness.a_lambda_field_of_a_generic_record_binds_the_type_its_sibling_field_determines
//   test.claim.generic_lambda_parameter_binding_witness.a_lambda_parameter_bound_to_a_type_variable_can_reach_its_fields
//   test.claim.import_admission_closure_membership_witness_test.listed_name_resolves
//   test.claim.import_admission_closure_membership_witness_test.pool_coincidence_name_still_resolves_today
//   test.claim.import_admission_closure_membership_witness_test.selective_unlisted_name_still_resolves_today
//   test.claim.operation_admit_callers_inert_witness.operation_form_admission_refuses_unadmitted_caller
//
// HOW TO READ SIXTEEN. It is an UPPER bound on census-caused miswarding and not a count of
// wrong rows: only the claim arriving FIRST at a given source absorbs the fill, so some of
// these are legitimately over the ceiling on their own marginal cost. It is also NOT a bound on
// the general class -- any row dominated by another unbracketed shared build is miswarded and
// invisible here.
//
// `both_closure_edge_index` IS NOT SUCH A BUILD, and this clause previously called it the
// obvious next candidate and left it unverified. Settled, and confirmed here by code read rather
// than relayed: `v1_compiler.cli_run` `run_required_floor` calls `warm_bare_reference_edge_index`
// TWICE during strict-preparation -- once over the source roots and once over
// `witness_layer_roots()` -- and reports the cost as `[floor-phase] phase=shared-index-warm`. So
// both index identities the fold can reach are built and billed BEFORE any claim window opens,
// which is why that artifact needs no bracket and why the ~28s it costs reaches no row on this
// roster. (Runtime confirmation on run 33135617689 is sleek-cat-45's; the warm sites are this
// carrier's own reading.) Naming it as a suspect here would have sent the next reader to measure
// a thing that is already correct.
//
// THE WELL-POSED SUCCESSOR IS NOT A ROSTER QUESTION, it is a construction one, and it is
// sleek-cat-45's framing: an artifact is either PREPARATION-FORCEABLE, in which case it is
// warmed and billed to preparation as the edge index is, or CLAIM-FORCED-ONLY, in which case its
// fill is netted out and the split reported as `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` does. The census
// memo was in NEITHER arm. That "neither" is a WRITABLE STATE is the actual defect -- gunbc#9560
// closes one instance of it and the next memoization reintroduces it.
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS MORE FOR THIS ROSTER THAN FOR AN ORDINARY MEASUREMENT: a row frozen for cost
// that belongs to a shared build cannot take the exit below, because the exit is "make it
// cheap" and the cost is not its own to reduce. It would sit here looking exactly like debt.
// TRIGGER: when gunbc#9560 lands the missing census bracket, RE-MEASURE THESE SIXTEEN and delete
// the lines that fall under the ceiling. That is a specific, dated, bounded action against a
// named list, which is the difference between a declared impurity and a silent one.

// HOW A ROW LEAVES, AND WHY THAT MAKES THE ROSTER SELF-CORRECTING. Delete its line. The floor
// then runs it under the 500ms ceiling and it either passes or fails on its own marginal cost.
// Nothing here needs to be re-measured to release a row, and nothing may be added to release
// one.

// RELEASED 2026-08-29, ONE ROW, ON A PRODUCER RECEIPT RATHER THAN ON A COST READING.
//
// `v2.test.languages_consumer_census.corpus.per_language_row_ratchet.corpus_per_language_row_ratchet_holds`
// leaves the proven roster. It is released because the cost it was enrolled for is now PAID
// SOMEWHERE ELSE, not because it was re-measured and found cheap -- and the distinction is the
// whole of the receipt.
//
// WHAT IT WAS ENROLLED FOR. The block above records it at cpu 645ms with "none" recorded fill in
// either `[floor-shared-fill]` format. That "none" was the tell and was read at the time as the
// row owning its cost: the row builds `languages_decl_records_cached`, a process-wide `OnceLock`
// over a token scan of every `.dag` and `.rs` file in the tree, and that build was NOT bracketed
// by `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu`, so `run_claim_measured` could neither net it nor report
// it. An unbracketed shared build is INVISIBLE to the marginal basis this roster is defined
// against -- it reads exactly like own work. This is the class the file already declares as its
// known bounded impurity, met a second time on a second artifact.
//
// WHY IT IS NOW ZERO. gunbc#9680 added `LanguagesConsumerCensusBuild` to `FloorPreparationPhase`
// and `required_floor_runner.rs` warms it by calling the declared producer once, after the
// prepared authority is installed and BEFORE any claim window opens, adjudicated by the same
// three limits as the other preparation phases. The artifact moved into the PREPARATION-FORCEABLE
// arm that the successor paragraph below names, so no claim is billed for it at all.
//
// THE RECEIPT IS AN EXECUTED PREPARATION LINE, NOT A COST COLUMN. Main run 33263972922, at
// 6515d6fb816 -- the first main run carrying gunbc#9680 -- announces
// `[floor-phase] phase=languages-consumer-census-warm state=completed cpu_ms=460 wall_ms=464
// rss_growth_bytes=0 decl_rows=72 provenance=built-by-preparation`. `built-by-preparation` is the
// load-bearing field: it says preparation found the memo COLD and paid the build, which is the
// only state in which this release is sound. Had it read `already-warm-on-entry` the fill would
// still have been paid by some claim and this row's release would have moved the bill rather than
// removed it.
//
// AND ITS OWN 0ms READINGS ARE NOT THE EVIDENCE, deliberately. The row reads cpu 0ms on main runs
// 33141550579 and 33145062452 and 642ms `completed_over_budget` on 33191118450, and NONE of those
// three figures is its marginal cost -- the 0s are borrowed from whichever row built the census
// first, and the 642 is the whole build billed to it for touching first. Measured in isolation on
// this branch, where it must be the first toucher, it costs 679ms; that number is the SIZE OF THE
// FILL, not the cost of the row. Releasing on a 0ms reading would have been the charged-basis
// error this file was rebuilt once to correct, in mirror image: there a real number belonged to
// another row, here a real zero does.

// THE 500ms CEILING ITSELF IS JUDGED UNDER CONTENTION, AND THAT IS NOT THIS FILE'S ROW TO STATE.
// Every release and hold reasoned about here compares a claim's cost against
// `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`, and the standing of that comparison -- its measured
// inflation, its bounded population, and the capability that restores it -- is carried by
// `gunbc.rung_drop floor_cost_claim_qualification_unavailable`. Cited rather than repeated: a second telling
// would drift from the authority that retires it.

// THE 45 test.claim.live_deploy ROWS ARE HELD, AND THEIR TRIGGER IS NOT A COST MEASUREMENT.
//
// This paragraph is where their trigger lives because the roster is a `List<String>` and carries
// no per-row field to hang one on; that is a real limitation of this carrier, stated rather than
// worked around.
//
// WHY THEY CANNOT BE RELEASED THE ORDINARY WAY. The exit above -- delete the line, let the floor
// run it under the 500ms ceiling -- presumes the floor RUNS it. After the 2026-08-29 gate cut
// these identities are outside the gate closure, so preparation never offers them, and the
// disposition projection now says so at identity grain (`DeclinedOutsideGateClosure`). There is
// also no full-corpus floor left in CI to measure them elsewhere: `.github/workflows/witnesses.yml`
// invokes `claim_executor --required-ci` twice, once per lane, and nothing else runs a floor. So
// their marginal cost is not observable by any instrument this repository currently runs, and a
// release would be bookkeeping rather than a measured shrink.
//
// WHAT IS KNOWN ABOUT THEIR COST, measured off-lane and recorded so the next reader does not
// re-derive it: the apply-script witnesses cost ~3.4s and the retract ones ~1.3s, and that is
// ~21ms per DISTINCT emitted bash command over a ~100ms base rather than anything about their
// byte sizes. The re-derivable form of that model is
// `v2.test.floor.bash_command_serialization_cost_model`, whose four K-varying rows carry it in
// `required_floor_claim_cost.tsv`. The 21 apply witnesses emit a BYTE-IDENTICAL script, so a
// cache surviving across claims collapses them to one payer -- which is why the repair is a
// same-key emission cache at the production owner and never a smaller fixture: the emitted
// command set IS what these witnesses assert, so shrinking it to buy the budget would shrink the
// subject.
//
// TRIGGER, and it is a CAPABILITY rather than a PR: a required population that reaches these
// identities -- the restoration of the DESIGN section 4b(3) rung drop "Required gate reduced to
// the compiler floor", by a change-derived required population rather than a static prefix
// roster. Until then they are held here, reported by the roster-standing line as
// `outside_this_runs_universe`, and explicitly NOT counted as debt. Deleting them on the strength
// of a cheap off-lane re-measurement would be the same error in the other direction as releasing
// a 0ms first-toucher row: a number that is real and is not the row's standing on the lane that
// owns the contract.

// NOT RELEASED, AND ITS TRIGGER NAMED SO THE ROW IS NOT RE-EXAMINED FROM SCRATCH.
//
// `v2.test.lens_inert_carrier.inert_carrier_test.inert_carrier_universe_is_nonempty` was examined
// in the same pass, on the same reasoning, and STAYS. It presents identically -- 0-1ms on runs
// 33141550579 and 33145062452, 1183ms `completed_over_budget` on 33191118450 -- and measures
// 1270ms in isolation on this branch. But its artifact, `inert_carrier_data`, has NO preparation
// warm: the block above already follows it across two runs by its `cache=inert_carrier_data
// paid_by=...` line, and the payer is simply whichever inert-carrier claim runs first. Deleting
// this line would not make the fill stop being paid; it would hand the 1.2s to a sibling that is
// under the ceiling today, which is precisely the regeneration this file measured once before
// (withholding 266 rows left 16 others tripping the same conjuncts, 9 of them under the ceiling
// on main, one at 1ms).
//
// TRIGGER, and it is a CAPABILITY rather than an artifact, per DESIGN section 4b(3): the row is
// released when `inert_carrier_data` is built and adjudicated in preparation -- a
// `FloorPreparationPhase` member warmed by calling its declared producer once before any claim
// window opens, reporting its own `provenance=built-by-preparation` -- OR when the build is
// bracketed by `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` so the marginal basis can net it. Either arm
// makes the row's own marginal cost knowable; nothing short of one of them does, and a re-measure
// on its own is not sufficient, because every figure the instrument can currently produce for
// this row is either borrowed or over-attributed. That build is not this lane's work.

// THE ROSTER CARRIES TWO POPULATIONS, ENROLLED FOR DIFFERENT REASONS AND NOT TO BE READ AS ONE.
// The figures in the two paragraphs below (225 and 51) are the counts AT ENROLMENT, not present
// sizes: each paragraph records what ONE measurement enrolled and why, so the number is part of
// that provenance and is correct as history. For the present sizes ask
// `floor_cost_debt_population_report`; re-pointing these two paragraphs at today's roster would
// destroy the provenance and create two more numbers to rot.
//
// POPULATION A -- 225 EXPENSIVE PASSES. Claims that REACHED A VERDICT, PASSED, and whose measured
// CPU exceeded `required_floor_claim_cpu_safety_limit_ms`. Read from main run 33141550579's
// per-claim cost receipt (`required_floor_claim_cost.tsv`, 12936 rows, `cost_basis=cpu`), so every
// figure is that run's own and none is transcribed by hand. Measured range 511..3757ms. For these
// rows the carrier's ordinary premise holds exactly: the witness works and costs too much.
//
// POPULATION B -- 51 ROWS TOO EXPENSIVE TO REACH A VERDICT AT ALL. 44 `budget_interrupted` on the
// CPU clock against 5000ms and 2 `completed_over_budget` on the wall clock against 10000ms
// (18882ms and 19024ms), measured on main run 33145062452 and supplied at identity grain by
// warm-tern-34. THESE ARE NOT CLAIMED TO PASS. An interrupted claim went UNDECIDED -- it may pass,
// it may fail -- and its reported figure is a LOWER BOUND describing when the deadline fired, not
// what the row costs. They are enrolled on cost alone, under the operator's standing instruction
// that work above the budget must not run: a claim that burns the entire ceiling and then answers
// nothing is the most expensive thing the fold can do, not an edge case to leave executing.
// Verified against the same cost receipt before enrolment: all 46 are `budget_interrupted` or
// `completed_over_budget` and NONE has outcome `fail`.
//
// HOW UNINFORMATIVE THAT LOWER BOUND IS, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED -- and the number is the
// point, because THREE READERS INDEPENDENTLY TREATED IT AS A POINT ESTIMATE. This carrier says
// above that a censored figure is a lower bound. It is, and the sentence is not enough: the bound
// and the true cost are essentially UNCORRELATED in this population, so any reasoning that sorts,
// buckets or thresholds on the reported figure is unsound even when every individual figure is
// honest.
//
// THE INSTRUMENT IS A PAIR OF FLOOR RUNS DIFFERING ONLY IN THE CEILING, so the join is a
// controlled measurement and not two observations of two trees. Censored: run 33191118450
// (`session/witty-wren-148`), 500ms CPU. Uncensored: run 33190645319 (`probe/ceiling-uncensor-9517`),
// ceiling raised, same tree. Both emit `required_floor_claim_cost.tsv`; the rows below are that
// artifact's own `cpu_ms` column joined on `identity`. The figures are transcribed here, as a
// declared exception to "name the instrument, never transcribe its output", for the reason this
// document grants that exception elsewhere: the probe branch is DELETED, so no producer on this
// side of the boundary can re-derive the uncensored half, and the subject of the row IS the
// divergence between the two runs rather than either run's state.
//
//   identity                                                          censored -> true (cpu_ms)
//   ..cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_dag_logic_has_three_fn_bodies      506 -> 2897
//   ..cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_file_row_totality_holds_on_smoke   506 -> 2907
//   ..cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_v2_ingested_body_is_not_decl..     502 -> 2902
//   ..identity_captured_navigation_roster_gate.green_control_sanctioned..    507 -> 2227
//   ..emit_on_demand_classical_not_ingested_family_witness.emit_on_demand..  502 -> 2374
//   ..emit_ingest_grammar_relation_round_trip.same_grammar_parse_ingest..    527 -> 1658
//   ..nominal_distinctness_cross_call.nominal_distinct_control_compiles_ok   505 -> 1596
//   ..lens_module_gate_witness.lens_closure_question_zero_holds_live        1655 -> 1713
//   ..external_model_scope_live_cover_witness.frontier_cover_of_live..       501 ->  559
//   ..operation_argv_corpus_witness.operation_argv_corpus_every_row_is..     502 ->  520
//   ..roadmap_belt_witness.witness_belt_plan_equals_step_composition         502 ->  355
//   ..roadmap_belt_witness.witness_belt_plan_paused_holds_on_authority       502 ->  380
//
// READ THE FIRST AND THE ELEVENTH LINES TOGETHER. `502 -> 2902` and `502 -> 355` are THE SAME
// REPORTED NUMBER. One row costs nearly six times the ceiling; the other is comfortably under it
// and was interrupted only because the fold's charged clock crossed the line during it. The
// reported figure does not distinguish them, and no amount of care in reading it can, because the
// information is not in the column.
//
// WHAT THAT REFUTES, NAMED SO THE READING DOES NOT RETURN. Two decisions on this axis were argued
// from the shape of this cluster, and both arguments are void:
//   - "membership is decided by run-to-run variance" (this branch's own PR body, over four
//     `bootstrap_footprint` siblings at 492/502/502/506ms). TRUE OF THOSE FOUR -- they measure
//     460ms uncensored -- and FALSE of the seven rows above them, which measure 1596-2907ms. The
//     reasoning could not tell the two groups apart, so it was right by luck on the group it was
//     applied to.
//   - "26 of 44 sit within 2% of the 5000ms ceiling, so scheduling noise decides them" (the
//     handoff for the 5000ms-era population, `calm-ram-380`). Same shape one ceiling up, and
//     the same refutation: those 26 rows' true costs are UNMEASURED and unbounded above.
//
// AND THE AGREEMENT WAS NOT CORROBORATION. Three readers reached the marginality reading
// independently, which reads as confirmation and is not: a shared input is a shared potential
// defect, and all three were reading one column that cannot express the distinction they were
// drawing. That is this document's own recognition rule applied to its own authors.
//
// WHAT THIS DOES NOT LICENSE. It is not an argument to move the ceiling -- if anything it is the
// opposite, since the rows nearest the line are the ones most likely to be far over it. It is not
// an argument to enrol or release any row here; the four `bootstrap_footprint` siblings measure
// under the ceiling uncensored and are NOT on this roster, which is correct. It settles one thing
// only: a censored figure may not be used as a cost, and a decision that needs a cost for an
// interrupted row must uncensor and re-measure, as the pair above did.
//
// THE CONSTRUCTION THIS WANTS IS NOT BUILT, AND THAT IS A DECLARED OMISSION RATHER THAN AN
// OVERSIGHT. `write_required_floor_claim_cost_tsv` renders a censored lower bound into the same
// `cpu_ms`/`wall_ms` columns a completed row's exact measurement occupies, distinguished only by
// the adjacent `verdict_reached` column -- which is an exact discriminator (false for exactly the
// 12 `budget_interrupted` rows and nothing else) that every reader above failed to join. Making
// the bound unable to inhabit the measured-cost slot is the DESIGN section 5 construction move,
// and it was deliberately not landed here: forward implementation is frozen under the 2026-08-28
// wind-down. TRIGGER: when that freeze lifts, a censored figure gets a rendering a numeric reader
// cannot silently consume, and this paragraph's warning becomes the regression control for it.
//
// THE ONE THING NEITHER POPULATION MAY CONTAIN IS A KNOWN FAILURE, and the reason is a live
// receipt rather than a principle. A first attempt at this roster was measured on THIS branch,
// with the 500ms ceiling already in place, and enrolled 276 interrupted rows. The tightened
// ceiling had converted six identities that FAIL on main into interruptions -- they did not stop
// failing, they stopped reaching a verdict -- so enrolling them would have laundered six live
// defects as cost debt AND removed them from the failure count that makes them visible. The
// instrument was masking the population it was being used to measure. Found by snappy-dove-250 by
// differencing the two failing sets, verified here at identity grain, and the roster was rebuilt
// from main's receipt instead. The ten known failures that also exceed 500ms stay OUT and stay
// visible; they run cheap, fail fast, and cost nothing worth reclaiming.
//
// WHY THE MEASUREMENT COMES FROM MAIN AND NEVER FROM THIS BRANCH: a cost roster cannot be measured
// on a tree that already carries the tightened ceiling, because the ceiling PERTURBS THE QUANTITY
// BEING MEASURED. Every one of those 276 interrupted members reported 501..505ms -- clustered at
// the ceiling, because the ceiling produced the number. Keying on that would have made the
// contract a fact about the limit's own value.
//
// THE BASIS IS MARGINAL CPU. The floor nets shared-artifact fill out of a claim's charged cost
// (gunbc#9477, gunbc#9560), so these figures are each row's own work and not the compile it
// happened to reach first. An earlier cut built on CHARGED figures was 37% wrong, and withholding
// a charged-basis payer does not even remove the cost: fill is paid inside execution on a memo
// that starts empty, so it moves to the next claim naming those sources, which then goes over and
// joins the roster in turn. A marginal figure is a property of the ROW, so declining a
// marginal-over row does not move THAT ROW'S OWN COST onto anybody else.
//
// THAT IS NARROWER THAN AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS FILE CLAIMED, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURED.
// It read "moves nothing to anyone and the population cannot regenerate itself". The second half
// is FALSE and the run that installed this roster proved it: withholding the 266 rows THE ROSTER
// HELD AT THAT RUN left 16 others tripping the same conjuncts, and 9 of them measured UNDER the
// ceiling on main -- one at 98ms, one at 1ms, one at 0ms. A witness that cost 1ms did not become
// expensive. It INHERITED A BILL. (266 is the roster size AT THAT MEASUREMENT and does not track
// the current size, which the header states. A historical measurement keeps the denominator it
// was taken against; updating it to today's figure would silently restate what was measured.)
//
// THE MECHANISM, AND IT IS NOT THE BRACKETED FILL -- a first hypothesis said it was, and the same
// run refutes that. Shared work is paid INSIDE execution by whichever claim reaches it first, so
// withholding the first-toucher moves the bill to the next claim naming that work. But only TWO
// call sites are bracketed by `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` (the diagnostic census and the
// rust emit check). Every OTHER memoised shared construction is relocated the same way and
// recorded NOWHERE.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATING OBSERVATION IS ON THE SIX ROWS THAT COMPLETED, whose marginal costs moved
// 89ms -> 59817ms, 98ms -> 25216ms, 1ms -> 1157ms, 0ms -> 645ms. Nothing got slower by 672x.
//
// THIS CLAUSE CARRIED A FALSE PREMISE ALONGSIDE THAT, AND ITS CORRECTION CUTS THE OTHER WAY. It
// said an interrupted row emits no per-claim fill line whatever it paid, so absence proves nothing
// there. The ordering behind that is right -- `record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu` is called after the
// uncached computation returns -- but the conclusion needs the deadline to be able to fire inside
// that window, and it cannot. The eval deadline is COOPERATIVE, polled by the interpreter, and both
// bracketed sites are straight-line Rust with no poll within:
//
//   let fill_started = thread_cpu_nanos();
//   let census = compile_dag_diagnostic_census_uncached(source);   // no deadline poll inside
//   record_shared_artifact_fill_cpu(thread_cpu_nanos() - fill_started);
//
// `compile_dag_rust_emit_check` is the same shape. A host builtin is ATOMIC with respect to a
// cooperative deadline, so a claim that enters a fill always finishes it and always records it.
// "Interrupted mid-fill with the counter unadvanced" is not a reachable state.
//
// SO ABSENCE OF A FILL LINE ON AN INTERRUPTED ROW IS A REAL ZERO, and warm-tern-34 finding none
// across all 44 is evidence those rows paid no shared fill rather than an artifact of the
// instrument. Population B's interrupt points are therefore NOT fill-inflated -- they are the rows'
// own charged cost up to the deadline -- so the enrolment rests on better evidence than this file
// was claiming for itself.
//
// THE CEILING LANDS INSIDE A DENSE BAND, AND THAT BOUNDS WHAT THIS ROSTER CAN ACHIEVE ALONE.
//
// Run 33191118450 confirmed the exhaustion argument: with all twelve `rest_path_reaches_no_cli`
// members withheld, `reference_edges` carries NO claim-paid key at all -- only `<outside-fold>`
// warms -- so the 25995ms fill STOPPED HAPPENING rather than relocating, and its payer left the
// interrupted set. Relocation is dissolved, not deferred.
//
// THE COUNTERS STILL ROSE, 11 -> 12 interrupted and 3 -> 5 over-cost, AND THE CAUSE IS UNRELATED.
// The newly tripping rows pay NO fill; the cost is their own. Four siblings in
// `v2.test.manual.bootstrap_footprint_anchor` measure 492 / 502 / 502 / 506ms against a 500ms
// ceiling. An earlier run recorded two of them at 512 / 502 and different members trip now. Which
// side of the line a row falls on is decided by a few milliseconds of run-to-run variance.
//
// MEASURED DISTRIBUTION, exact because the `[over-cost]` print is rank-ordered and these thresholds
// each admit fewer rows than its 25-row cap: 21 rows at or above 400ms, 15 at 450ms, 8 at 500ms,
// 5 at 550ms, 4 at 600ms, 3 at 1000ms. (The `>=100ms` figure is NOT readable from that print -- it
// saturates the cap. The population above the diagnostic cost line is the run's own
// `over_cost_line_diagnostic` counter, 701 on this run, and quoting 25 for it would be reading a
// print limit as a population.)
//
// SO PER-ROW ENROLMENT CANNOT CONVERGE HERE, for a reason that has nothing to do with the
// relocation this file spent most of its length on. Enrol the 8 that trip today and some of the 15
// at 450ms trip tomorrow; the roster would grow every run while no witness got slower and no cost
// changed. That is a treadmill produced by a THRESHOLD SITTING IN A CLUSTER, not by a mechanism.
//
// THIS FILE DOES NOT RESOLVE IT, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. The 500ms ceiling is an operator
// ruling and the band is new information about what that ruling costs, not grounds for this carrier
// to move the line by itself. Three responses exist -- enrol the whole band as a unit so membership
// stops being decided by variance; move the line clear of the cluster; or accept per-run
// re-enrolment. Only the first is convergent at this ceiling, and choosing among them is an
// operator decision this roster records rather than pre-empts.
//
// WHAT A COST ROSTER MUST NOT ABSORB, AND HOW CLOSE THIS ONE CAME. The floor's first completed run
// left 11 interrupted and 3 over-cost rows outside the roster, and the obvious move was to enrol the
// ones that looked expensive and stable. Eight were characterised that way, across three baseline
// runs, correctly. SEVEN OF THEM REACH A VERDICT AND FAIL.
//
// A 500ms ceiling does not create those failures; it changes how they PRESENT. A row interrupted
// before its verdict never reports the failure it would have reported, so a defect arrives dressed
// as a cost. Enrolling one puts a broken witness into a roster whose proven class asserts the
// witness WORKS and merely costs too much -- the debt contract then carries a correctness regression
// and reports it as budget. Joined at identity grain against main run 33145062452, SIX of the 11
// are in main's 47 unexpected failures: inherited defects belonging to the verdict-axis lane, not
// to this one.
//
// THE DECIDING FIELD IS `outcome`, NOT COST, AND BOTH READERS HAD IT IN FRONT OF THEM. deep-ant-102
// reported the outcome column in the same message that framed the set by expense, and this file's
// author was ready to act on the framing. Cost is what makes a row a candidate; outcome is what
// makes it eligible.
//
// THE FOUR ENROLLED FROM THAT RESIDUAL ARE VERIFIED AT A CEILING THAT LETS THEM FINISH -- run
// 33190645319, same tree, ceiling raised, nothing else changed:
//   operation_argv_corpus_every_row_is_materialized_or_counted  cpu=520   PASS
//   corpus_per_language_row_ratchet_holds                       cpu=646   PASS
//   inert_carrier_universe_is_nonempty                          cpu=1146  PASS
//   scan_denominator_is_a_real_corpus                           cpu=23794 COMPLETED_OVER_BUDGET
// All four reach a verdict. The first three pass and are caught only by the 500ms line; the fourth
// is a genuine 24-second row that is over budget at any ceiling anyone has proposed.
//
// THREE MORE WERE REFUSED ENROLMENT AFTER THE SAME CHECK, and their main-side disposition is why a
// row absent from main's 47 is not thereby new: `green_control_sanctioned_reader_body_not_flagged`
// and `nominal_distinct_control_compiles_ok` are KNOWN-RED on main, and
// `emit_on_demand_classical_not_native_one_build_holds` is NO-ROUTE. The 47 are UNEXPECTED failures,
// so an enrolled red and a hermetically-refused row are outside that population by construction.
// This branch introduces none of the three.
//
// PROVENANCE, because two readers were wrong in sequence and in opposite directions: crisp-newt-899
// first argued the emission site is unreachable for an interrupted row, then retracted that at
// source (the interrupt is a returned `ClaimOutcome::BudgetInterrupted` VALUE, not a process death,
// so the site IS reached). This file recorded the retraction correctly and kept a second, subtler
// form of the same error -- site reached, counter never advanced -- which the UNWINDING question
// cannot settle and the POLLING question can.
//
// AND THERE ARE TWO `[floor-shared-fill]` FORMATS, WHICH IS WHERE A FIRST READING OF THIS WENT
// WRONG. The per-claim form (`claim=... marginal_cpu_ms=... fill_cpu_ms=...`) is the one netted
// out of `cpu_nanos`. A SECOND, per-cache form (`cache=... fill_ms=... paid_by=...`) records who
// paid to fill a named cache and IS NOT SUBTRACTED FROM ANYTHING. An earlier revision of this
// block said all six emitted nothing and concluded the work was unrecorded; that was a regex
// reading only the first form, and swift-badger-524 caught it by counting both.
//
// MEASURED, the six split three ways:
//   `widened_scans_actually_read_the_tree`   cpu 59817ms, recorded fill 54267ms (reference_edges,
//                                            two payments) -- ~91% relocated, recorded, NOT netted
//   `inert_carrier_universe_is_nonempty`     cpu  1157ms, recorded fill  1160ms (inert_carrier_data)
//                                            -- essentially all of it, recorded, NOT netted
//   `scan_denominator_is_a_real_corpus`      cpu 25216ms, no fill recorded in either form
//   `corpus_per_language_row_ratchet_holds`  cpu   645ms, none
//   `bootstrap_footprint_*` (two rows)       cpu   512ms / 502ms, none
//
// THE MECHANISM IS OBSERVED END TO END ON ONE ARTIFACT, NOT INFERRED FROM MAGNITUDE. The floor's
// per-cache attribution names a payer, so the same artifact can be followed across two runs:
//
//   main run 33141550579   cache=inert_carrier_data fill_ms=1224
//                          paid_by=...inert_carrier_test.inert_carrier_no_unrostered_or_stale
//   this branch, 33152885910
//                          cache=inert_carrier_data fill_ms=1160
//                          paid_by=...inert_carrier_test.inert_carrier_universe_is_nonempty
//
// `inert_carrier_no_unrostered_or_stale` IS ENROLLED IN THIS ROSTER. It was withheld, and the
// identical bill -- same cache, same magnitude -- landed on its sibling in the same module, whose
// marginal cost went from 1ms on main to 1157ms here. Same artifact, same work, different payer,
// and the new payer is one of the six rows that then breached the ceiling.
//
// That is a withheld first-toucher's bill relocating, watched from both ends, and it is the
// evidence this file's non-termination argument rests on. Found by calm-ram-380 in main's own log
// while withdrawing a competing explanation; verified here at identity grain.
//
// THE SAME LOG SHOWS THE REMEDY ALREADY WORKING ON THE LARGEST ARTIFACTS, which is why the fix is
// a construction that exists rather than one to invent: `cache=module_path_index fill_ms=73088
// consumer_claims=454 paid_by=<outside-fold>` and `cache=module_graph_facts inclusive_ms=47737
// paid_by=<outside-fold>`. Seventy-three seconds across 454 consumers, charged to NOBODY, because
// the artifact is built before any claim runs. A warmed artifact has no first-toucher, so there is
// no bill to relocate and the population is stable under ANY ceiling. The open question is only
// which artifacts are outside that treatment, never whether the treatment works.
//
// SO THE UNIFYING FACT IS NARROWER AND SHARPER THAN "UNRECORDED": RELOCATED SHARED COST LANDS IN A
// ROW'S MARGINAL FIGURE WHETHER OR NOT IT IS RECORDED, because only ONE of the two recording
// mechanisms feeds the subtraction. For `widened_scans` the evidence is sitting in the log naming
// it as payer for 54 seconds it does not own, and its marginal figure still carries all 54. That
// is worse than the unrecorded case rather than better: the information exists and the ceiling
// does not consult it.
//
// WHAT THAT DOES TO THIS ROSTER'S DEFENCE: marginal keying prevents a row being enrolled for cost
// it never owned ONLY where that cost was netted as per-claim fill. Against per-cache fills and
// against genuinely unrecorded shared work it is inert, and a relocated bill is indistinguishable
// from own work at the instrument the ceiling actually reads.
//
// ENROLMENT TERMINATES PER KEY, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS BLOCK SAID IT COULD NOT. That
// revision read: "the fill must be paid by someone; there is always a first-toucher; so each
// round of enrolment produces a fresh population, and the process terminates only if the
// inherited fill falls under the ceiling -- which it cannot while the fills are multi-second."
// The first two clauses are true and the conclusion does not follow from them, because it treats
// the inheritor set as open.
//
// THE FIELD THAT DECIDES IT WAS IN THE LINE ALREADY BEING QUOTED. Every per-cache fill line
// carries `consumer_modules` beside `consumer_claims`, and the relocation receipt above quotes
// that line without reading it. Measured across both runs, EVERY claim-paid key has
// `consumer_modules` of 1 or 2 -- the consumers of a claim-paid artifact are one module, or two.
// So a relocated bill cannot leave that module, and enrolment terminates BY EXHAUSTION of the
// module's own membership rather than by the fill shrinking.
//
// WORKED ON THE KEY THAT PRODUCED THE CASCADE. `reference_edges`' four claim-paid keys report
// `consumer_modules=1`, all in `rest_path_reaches_no_cli`.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION SAID THAT MODULE HAS EIGHT MEMBERS AND WAS WRONG -- IT HAS TWELVE. The error
// was in the METHOD, not the arithmetic: the count came from the identities two floor LOGS happened
// to mention, and a log names the claims that RAN AND REPORTED, never a module's membership. The
// authority is the module's own source; `grep -c 'test fn'` on `rest_path_reaches_no_cli_test.dag`
// returns 12. Enrolling to eight was then claimed as CLOSURE, which was a coincidence of what two
// runs printed.
//
// THE NEXT RUN FALSIFIED IT EXACTLY AS THE MECHANISM PREDICTS: with eight withheld, the bill
// relocated to a NINTH member -- `dropping_the_definition_edge_roster_surfaces_more` -- interrupted
// before a verdict, in a module this file had declared closed. So the termination argument is SOUND
// and its INPUT was wrong. Exhaustion terminates, but only against real membership, and a
// population read off a log is not membership. That cost one 51-minute floor run to learn.
//
// THE MODULE IS NOW CLOSED AT 12/12, ENROLLED FROM SOURCE. The ninth member's own numbers are the
// argument: recorded `cost at least 28769ms`, of which `cache=reference_edges fill_ms=25995` is a
// bill four withheld siblings used to pay -- roughly 90%. It was not expensive; it inherited.
// `consumer_modules=1` on that key, so with every member withheld no claim reaches the artifact and
// the fill STOPS HAPPENING rather than moving to a thirteenth. That is what exhaustion buys, and it
// is only available because the membership came from `grep -c 'test fn'` on the module source.
//
// WHY THE FILL WAS INVISIBLE TO THREE TESTS THAT LOOKED FOR IT, recorded because the blind spot is
// reusable: that row's fill is `disposition=EXCLUSIVE`, and `disposition` is computed as
// `consumer_modules > 1`. The run's `shared_fill_ms=727` therefore bounds CROSS-MODULE relocation
// only -- a real figure, correctly summed, measuring a population this fill is not in. 25995ms is
// 36x that entire budget and contributes nothing to it. A paid_by-difference test over warmed keys
// cannot see it either (`<outside-fold>` in every run, so it cannot differ by construction), nor
// can a census of which REFUSED rows are shared (an inheritor is the SOLE payer, hence exclusive,
// and it completes or is interrupted rather than being refused). Three tests agreed and shared one
// blind spot; agreement among tests with a common blind spot is not independence. An
// earlier cut of this roster withheld six; the two it left were `widened_scans_actually_read_the_tree`
// and `withdrawing_the_consumer_admissions_surfaces_exactly_three`, and the branch run shows the
// ~26s bill landing on exactly those two and breaching in both -- 59817ms completed-over-budget
// and an interrupt at 27939ms. Found by calm-ram-380, who joined main's fill attribution against
// this roster and predicted both inheritors by name before the branch run was consulted. CLOSING
// the module takes all TWELVE members, which is what the source says and what a log cannot.
//
// THE BREADTH SPLIT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THE WARM IS NOT ALSO REQUIRED HERE. The keys with broad
// consumption are ALREADY charged to nobody: `module_path_index` fills for 70826ms across
// `consumer_claims=454 consumer_modules=109` and reports `paid_by=<outside-fold>`. Preparation
// warming is done precisely where breadth made a first-toucher unaffordable, and the keys still
// paid by a claim are the narrow ones, where exhaustion is cheap. Narrow means enrolment closes
// it; broad means the warm already did.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: that every cache in the corpus is narrow. This is measured over the keys
// appearing in these two runs. A key with BROAD claim-paid consumption would behave the way the
// deleted argument described -- each round finding a fresh payer in a different module -- and for
// such a key the warm would be the only fix. None appears here, and if one appears later this
// paragraph is the thing that must be re-measured rather than reused.
//
// HOW THE FALSE UNIVERSAL SURVIVED, RECORDED BECAUSE THE SHAPE IS GENERAL AND CHEAP TO REPEAT. It
// had TWO supports that looked independent and were not. One was a peer's general claim that the
// population re-forms every run and enumeration never converges. The other was this file's own
// relocation receipt, which appeared to confirm it. Neither reader had checked `consumer_modules`,
// and the receipt CANNOT confirm a universal: it shows one bill moving once, which is equally
// consistent with bounded relocation inside a closed module. So the second support was not
// evidence for the general claim at all -- it was evidence for the mechanism, promoted to
// evidence for the generalisation because it arrived beside one.
//
// THE RULE: agreement between a general assertion and a single receipt is not corroboration when
// the receipt cannot discriminate the general case from the bounded one. Ask what the receipt
// would look like if the universal were FALSE -- here, identical -- and if the answer is "the
// same", it supports nothing about scope. Both readers also quoted the very line carrying the
// field that refutes them, which is the tell: a datum adjacent to the one being cited, in the
// same line, unread twice in one night by two people.
//
// THE MOVE THAT ACTUALLY STABILISES IT is to stop charging the fill to a claim at all: build the
// artifact at PREPARATION and charge it to nobody, exactly as `module-path-index-warm`,
// `shared-index-warm` and `bare-reference-edge-index-warm` already are (each reports
// `provenance=built-by-preparation` and appears in no claim's cost). A warmed artifact has no
// first-toucher, so there is no bill to relocate and the population is stable under ANY ceiling.
// The artifacts behind this cascade never got such a warm. That is the repair; this roster is
// containment while it is absent.

fn floor_cost_debt_proven_chunk_00() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "gunbc.test.claim.realization_attempt_keystone_test.witness_assembled_cross_module_subject_reaches_typed_standing", tail: Cons { head: "gunbc.test.claim.realization_attempt_keystone_test.witness_assembled_identity_absent_refuses_RED", tail: Cons { head: "gunbc.test.claim.realization_attempt_keystone_test.witness_planted_garbage_refuses_frontend_RED", tail: Cons { head: "gunbc.test.claim.realization_attempt_keystone_test.witness_planted_minimal_module_emits", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.altra_contact_map_witness.w_ingested_contact_map_admits_at_declared_population", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.belt_tick_receipt_home_witness.the_belt_member_bootstraps_its_own_receipts_directory", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.belt_tick_receipt_home_witness.the_bootstrapped_directory_is_where_the_receipt_goes", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.lease_guard_sudoes_only_granted_binaries", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.locked_apply_guard_compares_the_modelled_prior_generation", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.locked_apply_guard_does_not_coerce_an_unreadable_store_to_zero", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.locked_apply_inner_program_is_quoted_not_spliced", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.locked_apply_script_serializes_generation_under_flock", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.fleet_converge_plan_witness.locked_apply_stale_diagnostic_keeps_observed_value_in_one_word", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.floor_attempt_standing_witness_test.w_RED_no_emitted_gate_arm_reaches_superseded", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.floor_attempt_standing_witness_test.w_RED_the_attempt_receipt_labels_are_one_authority", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.floor_attempt_standing_witness_test.w_RED_the_emitted_gate_renders_both_interruption_axes", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.floor_attempt_standing_witness_test.w_RED_the_head_axis_branches_on_event_type_not_an_empty_subject", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.host_axis_caps.witness_cap_author_before_shadow_revert_enforced_in_axis_caps", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.host_axis_caps.witness_cap_upsert_script_carries_no_hand_spelled_printf_quoting", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.host_axis_caps.witness_deploy_memory_cap_author_script_derives_allocation_bytes", tail: Empty {} } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } }
}

fn floor_cost_debt_proven_chunk_01() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.host_axis_caps.witness_padded_wrong_order_script_fails_author_before_shadow_revert_gate", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.host_axis_caps.witness_reversed_deploy_script_fails_author_before_shadow_revert_gate", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.apply.witness_digest_readback_script_is_bounded_and_post_mutation", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.apply_script_and_tree_sync_unit_stay_within_ci_runner_grants", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.emit_RED_unit_files_are_not_written_through_here_documents", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.emit_staged_unit_body_is_quoted_by_the_posix_authority", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.emitted_twin_apply_touches_none_of_productions_effects", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.emitted_twin_retract_removes_nothing_of_productions", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.fleet_converge_timer_not_installed_by_deploy_apply", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.production_apply_carries_its_own_handler_and_tree_sync_env", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.production_apply_creates_productions_binary_parent_directory", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.production_apply_serves_its_own_endpoint_and_scopes_no_path", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.production_belt_unit_carries_productions_spawn_root", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.production_retract_offs_its_own_endpoint", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.retract_is_not_emitted_without_an_observed_scoped_removal", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.retract_script_stays_within_ci_runner_grants", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.twin_apply_creates_its_own_binary_parent_directory_and_not_productions", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.twin_apply_serves_its_own_endpoint_and_not_productions", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.twin_belt_unit_carries_the_twins_spawn_root_and_not_productions", tail: Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.twin_retract_offs_its_own_endpoint_and_not_productions", tail: Empty {} } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } } }
