// THE THIRD EVIDENCE CLASS: STRADDLING (2026-09-04, gunbc#10366-era main).
//
// A STRADDLING ROW REACHES A VERDICT AND PASSES, ITS DETERMINISTIC WORK MEASURE IS INVARIANT
// ACROSS RUNS, AND ITS CHARGED CPU CROSSES THE 500ms CEILING ON SOME RUNNERS AND NOT OTHERS. It
// is neither of the two classes above and must not be filed as either: PROVEN asserts a measured
// marginal cost that EXCEEDS the ceiling, which is false of these rows on half their observations,
// and CENSORED asserts the row did not finish inside the ceiling on the charged clock, which is
// false of them on all of them -- they finish, every time. Filing a straddler as proven would put
// a universal over-ceiling claim on a row measured under the ceiling three runs out of five,
// which is the exact defect review 57258 refused when this file was one flat list.
//
// WHAT IS ENROLLED, AND WHY IT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT IS ENROLLED. Two identities in
// `v2.test.emit.produced_decl_two_target`. Their third sibling,
// `produced_decl_unwired_target_still_refuses`, STAYS ON THE FLOOR and is not touched: it is the
// same module, the same emission subject and the same shape of work, so it remains as the
// executing control that this module still runs, still refuses an unwired target, and is still
// measured every floor. A withhold that took the module is a module going dark; this one leaves a
// witness behind that fails loudly if the emission path breaks.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATING EVIDENCE IS `eval_steps`, NOT A CLOCK, and that column is the whole reason
// this class is separable from the two above rather than a softer wording of PROVEN. Three main
// floor jobs, read from their own `[over-cost]` lines:
//
//   run 33840229008 job 100920850069 (FloorRefused)
//     produced_decl_module_folds_declarations_in_order  eval_steps=170746  cpu=568  over
//     produced_decl_two_targets_render_own_order        eval_steps=197227  cpu=502  over
//   run 33841016028 job 100923146660 (FloorClean)
//     produced_decl_module_folds_declarations_in_order  eval_steps=170746  cpu=447  under
//     produced_decl_two_targets_render_own_order        eval_steps=197227  cpu=456  under
//   run 33840785132 job 100922476884 (FloorClean)
//     produced_decl_module_folds_declarations_in_order  eval_steps=170746  cpu=411  under
//     produced_decl_two_targets_render_own_order        eval_steps=197227  cpu=419  under
//
// The step counts are IDENTICAL to the unit across all three runs and the CPU figures move by up
// to 38%. The work the claim does is a stable property of the claim; the charge is not. That is
// `gunbc.rung_drop floor_cost_claim_qualification_unavailable` observed at identity grain, and
// this class is that row's population arriving in the one carrier that can act on it.
//
// WHAT WAS REFUSED, AND WHY, BECAUSE THE TWO EASY MOVES ARE BOTH WRONG HERE.
// RAISING THE CEILING is refused: 500 -> 600 tunes a literal so a check passes on a basis the
// declared drop says is unstable at ANY threshold, and buys nothing the next runner slot cannot
// take back at 602ms. It is also the tree-copied oracle DESIGN section 5 forbids -- the number
// would come from this same tree's current measurement.
// THE EXPECTED-RED ROSTER is refused, and the floor's own diagnostic refuses it for us in the
// sentence it prints beside these two rows: "This is a cost debt only -- it is not a defect and
// it does not belong on the expected-red roster, which asserts an expected FAILURE this row does
// not exhibit." Enrolling a passing row there would encode a false claim to buy a green.
//
// WHAT IS GIVEN UP, STATED SO NOBODY HAS TO INFER IT. Two passing emission witnesses stop running
// on ordinary required floors: that a two-target produced declaration renders in each target's own
// order, and that a produced-decl module folds its declarations in order. That coverage is GONE in
// steady state, not deferred. It is recovered on any change that TOUCHES these identities, which
// executes them under `ChangedCostDebtVerdictOnly` -- they must PASS, and the 500ms line is
// observed and published rather than gating -- so debt cannot smuggle an unverified edit to the
// witnesses it withholds.
//
// THE ADDITION IS DECLARED AGAINST THE SHRINK-ONLY DIRECTION RATHER THAN QUIETLY MADE. This
// contract's stated forward hole is that a NEW or REGRESSED row over 500ms simply refuses. These
// two are neither new nor regressed: `eval_steps` is unchanged run to run, so nothing about the
// witnesses moved. What moved is which runner the job landed on. An addition on that ground is
// the one the direction was never meant to forbid -- the alternative it leaves is a required lane
// whose verdict is decided by hardware -- and it is enrolled here, at identity grain, counted every
// run, and rostered as a DESIGN section 4b(3) drop at
// `gunbc.rung_drop.floor_cost_debt_straddling_produced_decl` rather than resolved by silence.
//
// TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY AND NOT A ROSTER ERASURE, AND EITHER ARM RETIRES THE ROW. (a) The
// produced-decl consumers stop paying to rebuild the rust `TargetModel` per claim -- the measured
// serve-below-recompute ON THOSE CONSUMERS SPECIFICALLY that `v2.workflow.floor_pure_producer_share`
// names as its own next trigger -- so the row's own work, not its luck, puts it under the line.
// (b) The floor qualifies a claim's cost on a measure that IS a stable property of the claim, at
// which point `floor_cost_claim_qualification_unavailable` retires and these rows are decided by
// their work rather than their slot. Deleting these two lines, emptying the roster, or shrinking
// the emission subject the witnesses assert over satisfies NEITHER arm.
fn floor_cost_debt_straddling_chunk_00() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "v2.test.emit.produced_decl_two_target.produced_decl_module_folds_declarations_in_order", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.emit.produced_decl_two_target.produced_decl_two_targets_render_own_order", tail: Empty {} } }
}

// THIS ROW WITHHOLDS REAL COVERAGE IN STEADY STATE. The claim remains authored, with two
// reversed-order controls and a missing-marker control, but is withheld from ordinary required
// floors while enrolled here. A change that touches the identity OVERRIDES that withhold: the
// claim executes, must PASS, and publishes its cost without the 500ms line gating the verdict.
// Thus debt cannot smuggle an unverified witness edit, while unchanged runs still lose this
// production-apply ordering coverage. The two repository-transition admission consultation
// witnesses execute on ordinary floors as well.
//
// THE COMPLETED CHANGED-DEBT VERDICT MEASURED THE COST honestly: 587ms marginal CPU, versus 563ms
// for `apply_script_and_tree_sync_unit_stay_within_ci_runner_grants` over the same production
// apply-script subject. The ordering predicate contributes about 24ms; the emitted subject, not
// the predicate, is the demonstrated cost boundary. Three earlier executions were preempted at
// the ceiling and supplied no cost measurement; those interrupt points are not reused as figures.
//
// TRIGGER (CAPABILITY, NOT ROSTER ERASURE): emission witnesses can reach a terminal verdict over
// the production apply script, either because that script becomes a shared fill-netted artifact
// or because an emission lane declares and enforces its own sufficient ceiling. Merely deleting
// this identity, emptying the roster, or replacing production emission with a smaller fixture
// does not restore the coverage and does not satisfy the trigger.
fn floor_cost_debt_proven_chunk_13() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "test.claim.live_deploy.emit.repository_convergence_is_ordered_after_binary_and_before_readback", tail: Empty {} }
}

// THE EVIDENCE CLASSES ARE SEPARATE FUNCTIONS, NOT ONE LIST WITH A NOTE BESIDE IT. Two are
// described here; the third, STRADDLING, is defined at `floor_cost_debt_straddling_chunk_00`
// above, beside the rows it holds and the runs it was read from.
//
// All are withheld and all take `DeclinedCostDebt`, because the DISPOSITION answers why a site
// is not planned and the answer is the same for each: it costs too much to run. What differs is
// the EVIDENCE, and review 57258 was right that a single flat roster let a universal claim about
// proven marginal cost stand over rows where no such cost was ever measured.
//
// PROVEN: reached a verdict, PASSED, and the measured marginal CPU exceeded the ceiling. For these
// the carrier's ordinary premise holds exactly -- the witness works and costs too much.
//
// CENSORED: the figure the floor enforced on is a CHARGED-CLOCK figure, so the row's own-work cost
// is UNMEASURED. Enrolling one asserts that it did not finish inside the ceiling on that clock --
// which is what the floor observed -- and asserts NOTHING about whether it would pass on its own
// work. TWO ROUTES REACH THIS CLASS and an earlier revision named only the first:
//
//   PREEMPTED -- the deadline cut in before a verdict, so the reported figure is an interrupt point
//   and the true cost can only be larger.
//
//   COMPLETED OVER THE WALL LIMIT -- the row finished, but shared-artifact fill is netted out of the
//   CPU clock ONLY. `wall_nanos` is left unadjusted and the wall completion check enforces on that
//   unadjusted figure, so a wall breach can be almost entirely borrowed fill. Two members are
//   exactly this: measured by warm-tern-34 on run 33160240865, their MARGINAL cpu is 0ms and 1ms
//   against fills of 18089ms and 17759ms, both `provenance=filled-shared-artifact triggered_by=ITSELF`.
//   Their own work is nil; the wall figure is the fill.
//
// THAT SECOND ROUTE IS AN ACCOUNTING GAP, NOT A PROPERTY OF THOSE WITNESSES, and this file says so
// rather than letting `DeclinedCostDebt` imply they are expensive. One netting rule with two homes,
// applied in one of them. cool-koi-235 is repairing it at the root by netting fill out of the wall
// clock as it already is for CPU; when that lands these two become cheap rather than invisible and
// their rows should be DELETED, which is the ordinary shrink this contract is built for.
//
// THAT SHRINK HAS NOW HAPPENED, AND THIS IS IT. gunbc#9609 nets shared-artifact fill out of the
// wall clock exactly as it already is for CPU, so the two rows below are cheap rather than
// invisible and they are DELETED from this roster by the exit path the paragraph above
// prescribes. Their own marginal work was 0ms and 1ms; what withholding was hiding was an
// accounting artifact, not a cost. Removing them returns them to ordinary execution with no
// disposition attached -- verified before the edit: neither identity appears in
// `v2.workflow.floor_expected_red`, `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap` or
// `v2.workflow.floor_non_verdict`, so nothing dormant wakes with them (`suppress_withheld`
// applies to all three rosters, and a deletion here makes any enrolment there observable again).
//
// AND THAT IS WHY THEY HAD TO LEAVE RATHER THAN SIMPLY BEING FIXED. While they sat here the
// floor reported `completed_over_cost_requirement=0` because they DID NOT RUN, which is
// indistinguishable in that counter from the repair having worked. The fix's own discriminating
// evidence -- those two rows reaching a verdict where they previously refused -- is only
// observable once they are back in the plan.
//
// THE PARAGRAPH BELOW IS KEPT IN THE PAST TENSE rather than deleted, because it is the reasoning
// that made the pre-emptive drop wrong at the time and it is what a later reader needs in order
// to tell this shrink from a premature one.
//
// THEY WERE NOT DROPPED PRE-EMPTIVELY, because at the time the floor enforced on the unadjusted wall and
// would refuse on them. Dropping a row whose cost is an artifact is right only once the artifact is
// gone; dropping it while the gate still reads the artifact just moves the red. What is corrected
// here is the CLAIM, not the membership -- an earlier revision described the whole roster as rows
// whose own marginal cost exceeds the ceiling, and for these two that is measurably false.
//
// CORPUS CONTEXT, from the same measurement and worth recording because it bounds the proven class:
// across all 12949 rows the MAXIMUM marginal_cpu_ms is 72, and only three rows carry fill above
// 5000ms. That figure is not extended to this roster's proven members here -- it was taken on a
// different tree and this file does not restate someone else's measurement as its own -- but it is
// the reason the second route above is worth naming rather than filing as an oddity.
//
// NO SECOND DECLINE ARM IS MINTED FOR THIS. A disposition names an admission outcome; evidence
// strength is a different axis, and giving the host a second arm to branch on would make it
// re-decide a question the roster already answers. The split is carried where the fact lives.
// THE GATE-BOUNDED SUBJECT (2026-08-29, #9663). Fourteen claims that completed inside the 500ms
// ceiling on the whole-tree subject are INTERRUPTED-BEFORE-VERDICT on the gate closure, on the
// GitHub runner and on srv2 alike, run after run (e8effe8, 1f4bda9, 92cc92e; srv2 lane 12).
// The subject changed what is warm before they run, not what they assert; the marginal cost is
// the gate's, and this roster is where a cost the ceiling cannot hold is declared rather than
// widened past. Same containment, same exit: a warm for the artifacts they first-touch. Two
// `lens_module_gate_witness` live claims were here for one run each: their whole cost was the
// 1.07s pool-root `module_path_index` fill (measured 1076ms with fill 1070ms), which the floor now
// warms in preparation from the declared producer, so they left as the exit was built. The four
// `bootstrap_footprint_anchor` rows are a different shape: 474-505ms CPU on three consecutive CI
// runs (0829ad8, 154fb1f, f462bc9 claim-cost receipts), no fill billed to them, so the ceiling
// itself is what decides them, run by run, by which runner slot the job landed on. That is not a
// verdict about the claim; it is withheld here until the ceiling stops deciding.
//
// THE WITHHOLD IS FOR THE ORDINARY FLOOR, AND CHANGED SELECTION OVERRIDES IT FOR THE VERDICT
// (FLOOR-CHANGED-COST-0, operator ruling 2026-08-30). This roster answers "should this witness
// run on every ordinary floor?"; when a change TOUCHES one of these identities its author is
// present and the floor must learn its verdict, so `v2.workflow.required_floor`
// `changed_witness_cost_policy` runs it under `ChangedCostDebtVerdictOnly` -- the 500ms CPU line
// observed and published against the debt identity, the wall deadline still armed, every
// non-verdict still red. THE ROW IS NOT REPAID BY THAT RUN and is not stale while it stands
// (`CostDebtWithholdOverriddenForChangedVerdict`): one favourable runner slot does not establish
// that a row observed through 505ms became safely ordinary, and this line leaves only through
// the explicit debt-removal transaction. The
// `non_fold_residue_no_unrostered_or_stale` row is the same shape: its whole cost is the
// `non_fold_residue corpus` scan it exists to check (475ms fill billed to it on the green run,
// 629ms CPU on srv2 lane 14 once the roster it reads carried its 22 missing rows), so the
// ceiling decides it by runner slot.
fn floor_cost_debt_proven_chunk_12() -> List<String> {
  Cons { head: "v2.test.lens_non_fold_residue.non_fold_residue_test.non_fold_residue_no_unrostered_or_stale", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.bootstrap_footprint_anchor.bootstrap_footprint_canonical_holds", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.bootstrap_footprint_anchor.bootstrap_footprint_non_canonical_violates", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.bootstrap_footprint_anchor.bootstrap_footprint_bad_content_hash_violates", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.bootstrap_footprint_anchor.bootstrap_footprint_bad_runtime_same_identity_violates", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.enforcement.cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_dag_logic_has_three_fn_bodies", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.enforcement.cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_file_row_totality_holds_on_smoke", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.enforcement.cost_coverage_witness.cost_coverage_v2_ingested_body_is_not_decl_facts_skeleton", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.identity_captured_navigation_roster_gate.green_control_sanctioned_reader_body_not_flagged", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.provenance.loaded_carrier_receipts.a_real_loaded_compilation_is_reachable_without_reading_the_live_tree_holds", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.provenance.loaded_carrier_receipts.a_record_type_declaration_does_not_reach_the_index_while_a_fn_does_holds", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.claim.provenance.loaded_carrier_receipts.function_bodies_are_token_level_in_the_normalized_carrier_holds", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.execution.emit_ingest_grammar_relation_round_trip.same_grammar_parse_ingest_bridge_holds", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.kotlin_grammar_claim.kotlin_grammar_parse_accepted", tail: Cons { head: "v2.test.manual.nominal_distinctness_cross_call.nominal_distinct_control_compiles_ok", tail: Empty {} } } } } } } } } } } } } } }}
}

